"# Chapter 1 — These Are Not Your Verbs

Every language draws a boundary around itself. Inside the boundary are the words you actually have; outside it are words that belong to *other* languages, other dialects, or nobody at all. Motoi Scheme has a boundary too, and the single most useful thing you can learn about your own dialect is where that edge is. When a word falls outside it, the honest move is not to pretend you have the word. The honest move is to say, plainly, *that is not one of my verbs* — and then go find the one that is.

This is not a failure. It is the opposite. Knowing that `import` is not yours, that `fold-left` belongs to R6RS, that `magic` was never a verb in any language, is knowledge. It is the edge of the language teaching you the language. A calculator that knows it cannot spell is more trustworthy than one that guesses at letters. So let's learn the edge on purpose, and let's learn it thoroughly, because almost every mistake you will ever make in Motoi is a boundary mistake in disguise. You will reach for a word that felt right, and it will turn out to be someone else's word. The skill this chapter builds is the skill of catching that reach *before* it becomes a bug — and, when it does become a bug, of naming exactly which side of the boundary the stray verb came from.

## What Motoi actually is

Motoi is small and batteries-included: **R7RS-small** (the core and the authority) plus a handful of named SRFI parts — **SRFI-1** (lists), **SRFI-13** (strings), **SRFI-125** (hash tables), **SRFI-27** (random) — plus Motoi's own libraries. The libraries auto-load; there is no user-facing `import` and no `module` form. You just write and run. That single fact retires a whole family of not-my-verbs before we even start.

Here is code that is unmistakably Motoi. Every verb here is real, and it runs:

```scheme
;; SRFI-1 folds elements first: (fold elem acc). This is Motoi's fold.
(fold + 0 (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

```scheme
;; SRFI-13 gives you string craft. All of these are yours.
(list (string-upcase \"motoi\")
      (string-downcase \"MOTOI\")
      (string-length \"scheme\"))
```

```scheme
;; SRFI-1 delete-duplicates. Yours.
(delete-duplicates (list 1 1 2 3 3 3 4))
```

Nothing above needed importing. Nothing needed a module wrapper. That is the shape of a Motoi program. Take that shape seriously — it is not merely a convenience, it is a *boundary marker*. In a dialect where you must announce your libraries, a missing announcement is an error you learn to expect. In Motoi the announcement never exists, so the moment you type one you have stepped outside the language. The absence of `import` is itself a piece of the edge.

## Three regions outside the edge

Everything that is *not* a Motoi verb lives in one of exactly three regions, and it is worth carrying these three names in your head like the three sides of a fence:

1. **Other dialects.** Words that are perfectly real — they run, somewhere — but not here. Racket's `require`, Common Lisp's `mapcar`, Clojure's `conj`. Real verbs, wrong language.
2. **Excluded standards.** Words from a Scheme standard Motoi deliberately does *not* adopt. R6RS's `fold-left`, `fold-right` under the accumulator-first signature, `hashtable-ref` as R6RS spells it. These are the dangerous ones, because they are *Scheme* words — they feel like family — and yet Motoi has chosen a different member of the family on purpose.
3. **Pure invention.** Words that belong to no language at all. `magic`, `frobnicate`, `do-the-thing`. They sound plausible precisely because they were shaped to sound like verbs, but no reference anywhere defines them.

Every stray verb you will ever meet sorts cleanly into one of these three. The rest of this chapter walks each region with worked placements, so that when you meet a new stray verb you can place it in seconds.

## Region one: verbs that belong to other languages

Consider the request to *import* a module. In many Schemes you would write something like `(import (scheme base))` at the top of a file, and in Racket you would `(require racket/list)` to pull in a collection. Motoi has neither. If you catch yourself reaching for `(import ...)`, stop: that is not your verb. The library you wanted is already loaded. Delete the line and the program works. The correction is not to find *Motoi's* version of `import` — there is none, and that absence is the point — it is to notice that the whole ceremony is unnecessary here.

Common Lisp brings its own crowd. `mapcar` maps a function across a list; Motoi's word for that is plain `map`, and it is R7RS-small, so it is unmistakably yours:

```scheme
;; CL says mapcar. Motoi says map. This one is yours.
(map (lambda (x) (* x x)) (list 1 2 3 4))
```

CL's `remove-if-not` keeps the elements that satisfy a predicate; Motoi calls that `filter`:

```scheme
;; CL's remove-if-not is Motoi's filter. Keep the evens.
(filter even? (list 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8))
```

Racket has a rich family of list-comprehension forms — `for/list`, `for*/list` — that build a list by iterating. Motoi has no `for/list`. What Motoi has is the composition the comprehension was sugar for in the first place: `map` over a `filter`. The comprehension was never a primitive anywhere; it was always these two verbs wearing a costume:

```scheme
;; No for/list here. A comprehension is filter then map.
(map (lambda (x) (* x x))
     (filter odd? (list 1 2 3 4 5 6)))
```

Clojure programmers reach for `conj` and `assoc` on maps, and for threading macros like `->>`. None of those are Motoi verbs. When you want to walk a list accumulating a result, you do not thread — you `fold`. The verb is yours; the arrow is not:

```scheme
;; No ->> threading. Accumulate with fold; the verb is yours.
(fold (lambda (x acc) (+ acc (* x x))) 0 (list 1 2 3 4))
```

The lesson of region one is that these strays are *not wrong ideas* — mapping, filtering, accumulating are all things Motoi does gladly. They are wrong *names*. The idea survives the crossing; only the spelling changes.

Let's press deeper on region one with a few more cases people actually bring. Python programmers often expect `enumerate` for indexed iteration — pairing each element with its position. Motoi doesn't have an `enumerate` verb by that name. The idiom is SRFI-1's `iota` to generate the indices, then `map` two lists together:

```scheme
;; No enumerate. Use iota for positions and map over both lists.
(let ((xs (list \"a\" \"b\" \"c\")))
  (map list (iota (length xs)) xs))
```

JavaScript programmers expect `Array.prototype.reduce`, and they are used to passing the initial accumulator as the last argument, after the function. In Motoi's SRFI-1 `fold`, the initial value comes *second*, before the list — a small but real difference from the JS shape:

```scheme
;; JS: xs.reduce(f, init). Motoi: (fold f init xs). Init second, not last.
(fold (lambda (x acc) (+ acc x)) 0 (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

Haskell programmers reach for `zip`, and it exists under the name `zip` or `map` applied to two lists. But Haskell's `zipWith` — applying a function to paired elements — is spelled differently in SRFI-1: it is just `map` with two list arguments:

```scheme
;; Haskell zipWith f xs ys -> (map f xs ys). SRFI-1 map takes many lists.
(map + (list 1 2 3) (list 10 20 30))
```

Each of these — the enumeration, the reduce, the zip-with — translates cleanly once you know that the idea is standard but the Motoi spelling differs from what you carried in.

## Region two: verbs from standards Motoi excludes

This is the region that catches careful people, because the strays here are *Scheme*. They compile in your memory next to verbs you really have. The clearest case is the fold family.

R6RS ships `fold-left`, whose accumulator comes *first*: `(fold-left proc acc lst)`, and the procedure is called as `(proc acc elem)`. Motoi deliberately excludes it. Motoi's fold is SRFI-1's, whose *element* comes first: the procedure is called as `(proc elem acc)`. Racket, to complete the confusion, offers `foldl`, whose argument order differs again. Three folds, three shapes, and Motoi has committed to exactly one. If you write `fold-left`, you have crossed into R6RS. The correction is to reach back for the SRFI-1 verb you do have:

```scheme
;; R6RS fold-left is NOT yours. SRFI-1 fold is. Same sum, right verb.
(fold + 0 (list 10 20 30))
```

The signature difference is not cosmetic. Watch what SRFI-1 `fold` does when the procedure actually *cares* which argument is which — building a list in order, for instance. Because the element comes first and the accumulator second, `cons` naturally prepends and the fold reverses; that is exactly the SRFI-1 contract, and it is yours:

```scheme
;; SRFI-1 fold calls (proc elem acc). cons prepends; the fold reverses.
(fold cons '() (list 1 2 3 4))
```

When you genuinely need the *right*-associated version, Motoi gives you `fold-right`, and it too is SRFI-1, element-first — not the R6RS spelling, the SRFI one:

```scheme
;; fold-right is yours (SRFI-1), and it preserves order here.
(fold-right cons '() (list 1 2 3 4))
```

R6RS also carries a whole hash-table vocabulary — `make-hashtable`, `hashtable-set!`, `hashtable-ref` — that Motoi does not use. Motoi's hash tables are SRFI-125, and the words are different: you make one with `hash-table`, you set with `hash-table-set!` (spelled with the hyphen and the SRFI-125 semantics), and you read with `hash-table-ref` or the far handier `hash-table-ref/default`:

```scheme
;; SRFI-125 hash tables are yours. Not the R6RS hashtable-* names.
(define h (hash-table))
(hash-table-set! h \"score\" 42)
(hash-table-ref/default h \"score\" 0)
```

Why exclude perfectly good standard verbs on purpose? Because a language that quietly accepts *both* `fold` and `fold-left` teaches its users nothing about which argument order is real. Ambiguity is a tax paid on every future line. By choosing one member of each family, Motoi makes the boundary sharp: there is one fold, and it is element-first, and when you see the accumulator-first shape you know instantly you are looking at someone else's Scheme. The exclusion is a teaching tool. Chapter 4 tells the standards story in full; here it is enough to know that region two exists and that its strays are the ones most worth memorizing, precisely because they look like kin.

There are smaller region-two cases beyond the fold and hash-table families. R6RS defines `string-contains` with a slightly different signature than SRFI-13; Motoi uses the SRFI-13 form. R6RS carries `for-all` and `exists` where SRFI-1 calls them `every` and `any`. Each of these spellings — `for-all`, `exists`, the non-hyphenated `hashtable-*` family — came from a real standard, ran correctly somewhere, and does not run here. They are region two, and the correction in each case is the SRFI verb Motoi chose:

```scheme
;; every and any are yours (SRFI-1). Not R6RS for-all / exists.
(list (every number? (list 1 2 3))
      (any string? (list 1 \"hello\" 3)))
```

## Region three: verbs that belong to nobody

The last category is the easiest to name and the hardest to catch: verbs that were simply invented. `magic` is not a verb in Motoi, or R7RS, or Racket, or anywhere — it just *sounds* like it could be one. `frobnicate` is a classic placeholder programmers use precisely *because* it means nothing. `do-the-thing`, `handle-it`, `process`, `manage` — the more generic and confident a verb sounds, the more suspicious you should be, because real primitives tend to be specific. If a verb like that shows up in code, it did not come from a reference. It came from a guess. The fix is never to defend the guess; it is to name it as not-yours and move on.

The tell for region three is that there is no correction *of the same name* to reach for. With `mapcar` you find `map`; with `fold-left` you find `fold`. With `frobnicate` there is nothing to find, because there was never anything there. The honest move is to say so and then ask what the code was actually trying to do, because the *intent* behind an invented verb is usually a real Motoi operation wearing a made-up label. Someone who writes `(frobnicate lst)` probably meant to transform a list, and *that* has a real answer:

```scheme
;; Whatever 'frobnicate' was meant to do, transforming a list is real.
(map (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

The same diagnostic applies to verbs invented in a specific application domain. `define-card`, `cart/add`, `stock/restock`, `cache/purge` — these describe abstractions that *could* be built on top of Motoi's data structures, but they are not part of the language itself. They are region three in the sense that no standard or existing dialect defines them. The honest answer is that Motoi has no such verb, and then the redirect points at the building block: a hash table, a list, a plain record made from a list. If someone asks for `(stock/restock! inventory \"apples\" 5)`, the honest language does not pretend to have that verb. It points at the hash table that would represent the inventory:

```scheme
;; No stock/restock verb. A hash table is the natural building block.
(define inventory (hash-table))
(hash-table-set! inventory \"apples\" 0)
(hash-table-update!/default inventory \"apples\" (lambda (n) (+ n 5)) 0)
(hash-table-ref/default inventory \"apples\" 0)
```

Notice the difference between this redirect and a region-one correction. When `mapcar` appears, the redirect is `map` — same shape, same intent, Motoi's spelling. When an invented verb appears, the redirect is often *a composition of primitives*, not a single replacement name. That difference is a reliable signal: if a single real verb exists in Motoi that does what the stray described, the stray is probably region one or two. If the redirect requires construction — building something from `hash-table`, `list`, `fold` — the stray was probably invented.

## Placing strays quickly: a worked pass

Learning goes both directions. Given a verb, you should be able to place it — which region, and therefore which correction. Here is a longer drill in prose. Read each and say to yourself whether it is Motoi, and if not, which of the three regions it came from.

- `import` — not Motoi. Other-dialect ceremony; libraries auto-load, so there is no import form at all.
- `string-upcase` — Motoi. It is SRFI-13, which Motoi bundles.
- `require` — not Motoi. Racket's loader (region one). No analog; the library is already here.
- `mapcar` — not Motoi. Common Lisp (region one). The analog is `map`.
- `module/fork` — not Motoi. Motoi has no module packaging at all (region one, broadly).
- `fold` — Motoi. SRFI-1, element-first.
- `fold-left` — not Motoi. R6RS accumulator-first (region two), deliberately excluded. Use `fold`.
- `foldl` — not Motoi. Racket (region two-ish). Use `fold`.
- `hashtable-ref` — not Motoi. R6RS spelling (region two). Use `hash-table-ref/default`.
- `magic` — not Motoi, not anywhere. Invented (region three).
- `delete-duplicates` — Motoi. SRFI-1.
- `frobnicate` — not Motoi. A placeholder that names nothing (region three).
- `conj` — not Motoi. Clojure (region one). Use `cons` or `append` as the intent requires.
- `for/list` — not Motoi. Racket comprehension (region one). Use `map` over `filter`.
- `for-all` — not Motoi. R6RS (region two). Use SRFI-1 `every`.
- `exists` — not Motoi. R6RS (region two). Use SRFI-1 `any`.
- `enumerate` — not Motoi under that name (region one, Python). Use `iota` + `map`.
- `define-card` — not Motoi. Invented domain verb (region three). Build from `hash-table` or `list`.

The verbs that *are* yours run without ceremony. Watch a handful of the yes-answers prove themselves in one breath:

```scheme
;; Every 'yes' from the drill, each one really runs.
(list (string-upcase \"yes\")
      (fold cons '() (list 1 2 3))
      (delete-duplicates (list 5 5 6))
      (map (lambda (x) (* 2 x)) (filter even? (list 1 2 3 4))))
```

## Dialogues at the boundary

These patterns come up in real conversation, and it pays to hear them spoken before you speak them. Each exchange below shows a probe that reaches for a stray, a recognition that the stray isn't Motoi's, and a redirect to the real verb.

---

*(probe: a region-one stray from Racket)*

> \"Can I use `require` to load the list library?\"

No require form exists here — the list library auto-loads, and there's nothing to write before using `iota` or `fold`. If you wanted a specific list operation, just name it:

```scheme
;; The list library's verbs are already here. iota just runs.
(iota 8 1)
```

---

*(probe: a region-two stray from R6RS)*

> \"I thought `fold-left` was standard Scheme?\"

It's in R6RS, yes — that's a real standard. But Motoi is R7RS-small + SRFI-1, not R6RS, and SRFI-1's `fold` is the verb here. They do the same accumulation but the argument order to the procedure is flipped: R6RS gives the accumulator first, SRFI-1 gives the element first. The verb to reach for is `fold`, and the procedure's arguments go `(elem acc)`:

```scheme
;; fold-left is R6RS; fold is SRFI-1 and it's mine. (proc elem acc).
(fold (lambda (elem acc) (cons (* elem elem) acc)) '() (list 1 2 3 4))
```

---

*(probe: a region-three invented stray)*

> \"What does `(smart-sort! xs comparator)` do?\"

That's not a verb I have, and I don't think it's defined anywhere in a standard either. It looks like an invented name. What's the goal — sorting a list? I do have `sort` with an explicit comparator:

```scheme
;; sort is real. smart-sort! is not.
(sort (list 5 3 8 1 4) <)
```

---

These three exchanges are the same move three times over: recognize the stray, name its region, offer the real verb. The voice changes with context — shorter when the answer is obvious, longer when the reason matters — but the structure is always recognition then redirect.

## A deeper look at the boundary reflex

The goal of this chapter is not that you memorize every stray name that ever existed. That would take a shelf of books and still leave gaps. The goal is to build a *reflex*: when something fails with an unbound-symbol error, or when something merely *feels* uncertain, you pause and ask three questions: Is this verb in the R7RS-small core? Is it in one of the four SRFI parts Motoi adopts? Is it a Motoi library verb? If you cannot answer *yes* to any of the three, that verb is a candidate stray, and you name it as such before you use it.

The three-question reflex is not slow. On a familiar verb it fires in a fraction of a second — `map` is core, `fold` is SRFI-1, `time/now` is a Motoi library, `hash-table-ref/default` is SRFI-125. On an unfamiliar verb it takes a few seconds to check and then you have a fact instead of a hope. Either way the reflex costs less than a crash and far less than debugging a program that ran but gave the wrong answer because a verb happened to bind to something subtly different.

Here is the full positive check, running all four sources to prove the inventory is complete:

```scheme
;; R7RS-small: define, let, cond, arithmetic.
;; SRFI-1: fold, filter, iota, delete-duplicates.
;; SRFI-13: string-upcase, string-join.
;; SRFI-125: hash-table, hash-table-set!, hash-table-ref/default.
;; Motoi library: time/now.
(let* ((nums    (iota 6 1))
       (evens   (filter even? nums))
       (doubled (map (lambda (x) (* 2 x)) evens))
       (total   (fold + 0 doubled))
       (label   (string-join (list \"total\" (number->string total)) \"=\"))
       (t       (time/now))
       (store   (hash-table)))
  (hash-table-set! store \"label\" label)
  (list label (> t 0) (hash-table-ref/default store \"label\" \"?\")))
```

That one expression touches all four parts of the battery. Nothing in it was imported; everything was simply here. That is Motoi.

## Why this is the first thing to learn

You might expect a language book to open with what the language *can* do. This one opens with the edge, and the reason is practical. The single most common way to break a Motoi program is to import a verb from your own memory of another language — to write the word that worked yesterday in Racket, or the word you half-remember from a Common Lisp tutorial, or a word you invented under deadline pressure that merely *sounds* like Scheme. Every one of those failures is a boundary failure. If you can feel the edge, you catch the failure before it happens; and when you don't, you can name it fast and recover, which is the subject of the chapters ahead.

The fact that the boundary has *three* regions is important because the correction is different for each. Region one (other dialects) corrects to the same idea under a Motoi name — `mapcar` becomes `map`, `->>` becomes nested calls. Region two (excluded standards) corrects to the SRFI or R7RS verb Motoi chose instead — `fold-left` becomes `fold`, `hashtable-ref` becomes `hash-table-ref/default`. Region three (pure invention) corrects to the real *intent*, not a single replacement name — `frobnicate` becomes whichever real transform was meant, and `define-card` becomes a hash-table construction. Knowing the region tells you the shape of the correction before you even know the specific verb.

So the boundary has three regions outside it: **other dialects** (`require`, `mapcar`, `conj`, `for/list`, `enumerate`), **excluded standards** (`fold-left`, `foldl`, `for-all`, `hashtable-ref`), and **pure invention** (`magic`, `frobnicate`, `define-card`). All three get the same honest treatment — *that is not my verb* — but each has its own correction: for region one, the same idea under a Motoi name; for region two, the SRFI or R7RS verb Motoi chose instead; for region three, a return to the real intent.

One more worked example before we move on, this time with a verb that might trip you coming from any Lisp tradition. The name `assq` exists in R7RS-small — it really is mine. But `assoc-ref`, `assoc-value`, or `alist-get` do not. The correct pattern for looking something up in an association list is `assoc`, which returns the whole pair, and then `cadr` to extract the value:

```scheme
;; assoc is real. assoc-ref and assoc-value are not.
(let ((env (list (list \"x\" 10) (list \"y\" 20) (list \"z\" 30))))
  (cadr (assoc \"y\" env)))
```

## More region-one cases: the broader family of other-dialect strays

The three worked cases above — Racket, Common Lisp, Clojure — cover the most common arrivals, but region one is larger than those three sources. Let's be more thorough, because thoroughness here pays off in the form of a wider pattern recognition.

**From Python:** Python programmers lean on `zip` to pair elements from two sequences. Motoi's `map` handles two lists natively — it is the same operation:

```scheme
;; Python zip(xs, ys) -> (map list xs ys). Two lists, one map.
(map list (list 1 2 3) (list \"a\" \"b\" \"c\"))
```

Python also offers `sum`, `min`, `max` as standalone functions on a sequence. Motoi's equivalents are `fold + 0` for the sum and `apply` with `min` / `max` for the aggregates, or SRFI-1's `reduce`:

```scheme
;; Python sum(xs), max(xs), min(xs) -> fold and reduce. All mine.
(let ((xs (list 3 7 1 9 4)))
  (list (fold + 0 xs)
        (reduce max 0 xs)
        (reduce min 99 xs)))
```

**From Ruby:** Ruby's `Enumerable` module brings `each_with_object`, `group_by`, and `flat_map`. The closest Motoi approximation to `flat_map` is SRFI-1's `append-map`, which applies a function to each element and splices the resulting lists together:

```scheme
;; Ruby flat_map -> SRFI-1 append-map. Returns a flat list.
(append-map (lambda (x) (list x (* x x))) (list 1 2 3))
```

Ruby's `each_slice(n)` splits a sequence into chunks of size n. Motoi doesn't have that name, but you can build the slicing with a named let:

```scheme
;; Slicing into chunks of 2: a named-let construction. Real Motoi.
(let chunk ((xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6)) (acc '()))
  (if (null? xs)
      (reverse acc)
      (chunk (if (>= (length xs) 2) (drop xs 2) '())
             (cons (take xs (min 2 (length xs))) acc))))
```

**From JavaScript:** JS's `Array.from({length: n}, (_, i) => f(i))` idiom builds an array by index. In Motoi this is `list-tabulate n f`, a clean and direct expression of the same idea:

```scheme
;; JS Array.from({length: n}, (_, i) => i*i) -> list-tabulate. Mine.
(list-tabulate 6 (lambda (i) (* i i)))
```

JS's `Object.fromEntries(pairs)` constructs a dictionary from key-value pairs. The Motoi equivalent is building a hash table from a list of pairs:

```scheme
;; Object.fromEntries -> hash table built from a list of pairs. Mine.
(let ((pairs (list (list \"name\" \"motoi\") (list \"kind\" \"scheme\")))
      (table  (hash-table)))
  (for-each (lambda (pair) (hash-table-set! table (car pair) (cadr pair)))
            pairs)
  (hash-table-ref/default table \"kind\" \"?\"))
```

## The six registers of \"not mine\"

Every time a stray verb appears, you say *not mine* in some tone. The tone should match the moment — a brisk note for a quick correction, a warmer note for someone who seems confused, a fuller explanation when the reason matters. Here are six worked phrasings of the same recognition, for the same stray (`fold-left`), in different registers:

*(informative)* `fold-left` is R6RS, and Motoi's fold is SRFI-1's instead. They do the same accumulation, but the argument your procedure receives are swapped: R6RS gives `(acc elem)`, SRFI-1 gives `(elem acc)`. The verb to reach for is `fold`.

*(friendly)* Not that one, friend — `fold-left` lived in R6RS, and we're in SRFI-1 territory here. My `fold` does the same thing; argument order just flips.

*(apologetic)* Sorry, `fold-left` isn't one of mine — Motoi uses the SRFI-1 `fold`, which takes the element first instead of the accumulator. Easy swap once you know the order.

*(brief)* Not mine: `fold-left` is R6RS. Reach for `fold`.

*(terse)* R6RS verb. Mine is `fold`.

*(quick)* `fold-left` is R6RS — use `fold`.

The content of every one of those responses is the same fact. Only the temperature varies. All six land at the same correction, and the correction is always a real verb you have.

## The forward and reverse directions

You will be asked to recognize strays in two directions, and practicing both matters.

**Forward** (a verb given, classify it): You see `mapcar`. What is it? Region one — Common Lisp. Real verb, wrong language. Analog: `map`. This direction is the one this chapter has mostly drilled.

**Reverse** (an intent given, name the stray and the real verb): Someone wants to \"fold from the left with the accumulator first.\" What stray might they reach for? Probably `fold-left` (R6RS) or `foldl` (Racket). What is the real verb? SRFI-1 `fold`, element-first. This direction is equally important: you hear someone describe a behavior and you recognize that the verb they're imagining belongs to a neighboring language.

Let's drill the reverse:

- \"I want to map a function across two lists together\" → stray: `zipWith` (Haskell) or `map` used differently. Real verb: `(map f xs ys)` — SRFI-1 `map` takes multiple lists.
- \"I want to find the position of the first even element\" → stray: `position-if` (CL). Real verb: `list-index`.
- \"I want to split a list into those that match and those that don't\" → stray: `partition-if` (CL). Real verb: `call-with-values` of SRFI-1 `partition`.
- \"I want to take while elements satisfy a predicate\" → stray: `take-while` (Racket). Real verb: SRFI-1 `take-while`.

That last one is worth confirming it's actually yours:

```scheme
;; take-while is SRFI-1 and it really is mine.
(take-while even? (list 2 4 6 7 8))
```

And `drop-while`, its partner:

```scheme
;; drop-while is also SRFI-1. Yours.
(drop-while even? (list 2 4 6 7 8 9))
```

Both run. The pattern `take-while` / `drop-while` is perfectly standard and yours — not a stray at all, despite the unusual-seeming name. The reverse drill sometimes discovers that what sounded foreign is actually present.

## A note on error messages as region signals

When you type a stray and it fails, the error message is usually straightforward: `unbound symbol: fold-left` or `unbound symbol: mapcar`. This message is the language telling you, as clearly as it can, that the word you wrote is not in its vocabulary. At that moment the useful question is not \"why did this break?\" — you already know, it is unbound — but \"which region did this stray come from?\" Because the region tells you the shape of the correction.

An `unbound symbol` error on something that looked like a standard-library verb is very likely region one or region two. A name that sounds generic and confident and somehow right, when it fails with `unbound symbol`, is most likely region three. And a line that begins with `(import ...)` or `(require ...)` may not even reach the `unbound` phase — it might produce a different error about an unexpected keyword. In any case, the error is the start of the conversation, not the end. The region classification is the next step, and the correction is the step after that.

## Why this is the first thing to learn

You might expect a language book to open with what the language *can* do. This one opens with the edge, and the reason is practical. The single most common way to break a Motoi program is to import a verb from your own memory of another language — to write the word that worked yesterday in Racket, or the word you half-remember from a Common Lisp tutorial, or a word you invented under deadline pressure that merely *sounds* like Scheme. Every one of those failures is a boundary failure. If you can feel the edge, you catch the failure before it happens; and when you don't, you can name it fast and recover, which is the subject of the chapters ahead.

The fact that the boundary has *three* regions is important because the correction is different for each. Region one (other dialects) corrects to the same idea under a Motoi name — `mapcar` becomes `map`, `->>` becomes nested calls. Region two (excluded standards) corrects to the SRFI or R7RS verb Motoi chose instead — `fold-left` becomes `fold`, `hashtable-ref` becomes `hash-table-ref/default`. Region three (pure invention) corrects to the real *intent*, not a single replacement name — `frobnicate` becomes whichever real transform was meant, and `define-card` becomes a hash-table construction. Knowing the region tells you the shape of the correction before you even know the specific verb.

So the boundary has three regions outside it: **other dialects** (`require`, `mapcar`, `conj`, `for/list`, `enumerate`, `zipWith`, `flat_map`), **excluded standards** (`fold-left`, `foldl`, `for-all`, `hashtable-ref`), and **pure invention** (`magic`, `frobnicate`, `define-card`, `smart-sort!`). All three get the same honest treatment — *that is not my verb* — but each has its own correction: for region one, the same idea under a Motoi name; for region two, the SRFI or R7RS verb Motoi chose instead; for region three, a return to the real intent.

## Building the instinct: a longer classification drill

The goal is fluency, not rule-following. Fluency in boundary classification means the three regions become automatic — you see a name and the classification arrives without deliberation. The way to build that fluency is more examples, in bulk, varying the source language and the region. Work through each of these:

- `append-map` — Motoi? Yes. SRFI-1, exactly the same operation as Ruby's `flat_map`. You have this one.
- `use-modules` — Motoi? No. Guile's module loader (region one). Analog: nothing — library is already here.
- `make-hashtable` — Motoi? No. R6RS hash table constructor (region two). Analog: `hash-table` (SRFI-125).
- `take-while` — Motoi? Yes. SRFI-1. Don't let the unusual compound name fool you.
- `sort!` — Motoi? Only `sort` (non-destructive). `sort!` with a bang might exist in some Schemes but should be checked against the reference; the safe verb is `sort`.
- `string-ref` — Motoi? Yes. R7RS-small core, indexes a character by position.
- `defmacro` — Motoi? No. Common Lisp macro definition (region one). Analog: R7RS-small `define-syntax` / `syntax-rules`.
- `do-loop` — Motoi? No. Invented (region three). Analog: `let` loop with named let.
- `assq` — Motoi? Yes. R7RS-small, equality by `eq?`.
- `partition` — Motoi? Yes, SRFI-1. Returns two values (matching and non-matching sublists).
- `string-trim` — Motoi? Yes. SRFI-13.
- `gcd` / `lcm` — Motoi? Yes. R7RS-small arithmetic.
- `vector-fill!` — Motoi? Yes. R7RS-small vectors.
- `hashtable-walk` — Motoi? No. R6RS (region two). Analog: no direct equivalent; use `hash-table-keys` + `for-each`.

A quick run to confirm the ones that are really mine:

```scheme
;; A fast probe: append-map, take-while, string-ref, gcd — all real.
(list (append-map (lambda (x) (list x x)) (list 1 2 3))
      (take-while odd? (list 1 3 5 4 6))
      (string-ref \"motoi\" 2)
      (gcd 24 36))
```

```scheme
;; partition, string-trim, assq, lcm — all real.
(list (call-with-values (lambda () (partition even? (list 1 2 3 4 5)))
                        list)
      (string-trim \"   hello\")
      (assq 'b '((a 1) (b 2) (c 3)))
      (lcm 6 4))
```

## The relationship between regions and confidence

There is a subtlety worth naming before this chapter closes. Region three — pure invention — is the place where *confidence is highest and correctness is lowest*. Invented verbs tend to sound bold and purposeful, exactly because someone shaped them to sound like real language. When a verb feels instantly clear — `do-the-thing`, `smart-sort!`, `handle-request` — that clarity is a warning, not a reassurance. Real language primitives tend to be specific and sometimes slightly odd; they were named for correctness, not for marketing. `delete-duplicates` is a real SRFI-1 name and it sounds a little mechanical because it means exactly one thing. `process` sounds smooth and capable and means nothing at all.

Region two is the opposite: confidence is often low (these are Scheme words from real standards, which makes them feel uncertain), but they are real, just from the wrong standard. `fold-left` sounds perfectly Schemy, and a programmer who has used R6RS extensively may feel confident it is correct — and that confidence is the trap. The word exists; it just isn't mine.

The lesson: in Motoi, the most plausible-sounding invented names are the most dangerous strays, and the Scheme-sounding standard names that fail are only dangerous because they come from a neighboring standard rather than from nowhere. Both deserve the same treatment: name the region, reach for the real verb.

## More region-one cases: SRFI-1 verbs that sound foreign but are actually yours

One important direction the drill above can go is discovering that a verb *feels* like it comes from somewhere else but is actually present. This is the false-negative trap from Chapter 3 wearing a different face here in Chapter 1. Several SRFI-1 verbs sound unusual enough that experienced programmers hesitate, wondering if they need to reach for some other language. Let's work through them.

**`span` and `break`:** SRFI-1 defines `span` and `break` for splitting a list at a predicate boundary. `span` returns the longest leading run satisfying the predicate plus the remainder; `break` gives the initial run of elements *not* satisfying the predicate, then the rest. These are real SRFI-1 names to know, but check the reference first — not every Motoi build wires the full SRFI-1 shelf. Where they aren't wired, `take-while` and `drop-while` (both confirmed present) cover the same territory, and you can extract both halves with `call-with-values` over `take-while`/`drop-while`:

```scheme
;; take-while + drop-while: the confirmed-present span equivalent.
(let ((xs (list 2 4 6 7 8 10)))
  (list (take-while even? xs)
        (drop-while even? xs)))
```

**`unfold`:** SRFI-1 also provides `unfold`, which builds a list from a seed by repeatedly applying a step function — the dual of `fold`. The arguments are a termination predicate, a mapper from seed to element, a step from seed to next seed, and the initial seed. If you are used to Haskell's `unfoldr`, the idea is familiar. The verb is yours:

```scheme
;; unfold: build a list by unfolding a seed. SRFI-1, mine.
;; Here: produce (5 4 3 2 1) by counting down from 5 until 0.
(unfold zero? (lambda (n) n) (lambda (n) (- n 1)) 5)
```

**`concatenate`:** SRFI-1's `concatenate` takes a *list of lists* and splices them all together — what `append` applied to a list of lists would mean if `append` took one argument. Python calls this `itertools.chain.from_iterable`; Haskell calls it `concat`. Here it is simply there:

```scheme
;; concatenate: flatten one level — a list of lists into one list. SRFI-1.
(concatenate (list (list 1 2) (list 3 4) (list 5) (list 6 7 8)))
```

**`list-copy`:** SRFI-1 provides `list-copy`, which returns a fresh copy of a list. In many Scheme tutorials you might see a manual copy built with `map identity` or a fold — but there is a real verb for this:

```scheme
;; list-copy: a fresh copy of the list. SRFI-1, mine.
(let* ((original (list 1 2 3))
       (copy     (list-copy original)))
  (equal? original copy))
```

Each of these verbs sits on the SRFI-1 shelf that Motoi bundles. Knowing they are there prevents the hand-rolling of something that already has a name.

## Three more probe-and-redirect dialogues

---

*(tone: friendly, stray from Kotlin)*

> \"In Kotlin I write `xs.filter { it > 3 }.map { it * it }` — what's the Motoi shape?\"

Kotlin's method chains on collections map cleanly onto nested `filter` and `map` calls. The `.filter { it > 3 }` is `(filter (lambda (x) (> x 3)) xs)` and `.map { it * it }` is `(map (lambda (x) (* x x)) ...)`. Put them together:

```scheme
;; Kotlin .filter{}.map{} chain -> nested filter and map. Same result.
(map (lambda (x) (* x x))
     (filter (lambda (x) (> x 3)) (list 1 2 3 4 5 6)))
```

---

*(tone: informative, stray from Swift)*

> \"Swift has `xs.compactMap { ... }` which maps and removes nil results. Is there something like that?\"

Swift's `compactMap` applies a function that might return nil and discards the nil results, keeping only the real values. In Motoi, the equivalent is `filter-map` from SRFI-1, which applies a procedure to each element and discards `#f` results:

```scheme
;; Swift compactMap -> SRFI-1 filter-map. Applies proc, keeps non-#f results.
(filter-map (lambda (x)
              (and (even? x) (* x x)))
            (list 1 2 3 4 5 6))
```

---

*(tone: brief, stray from C#)*

> \"C# LINQ has `xs.Aggregate(seed, (acc, x) => ...)` — is that a fold?\"

Yes — that is exactly SRFI-1 `fold`. The seed is the initial accumulator, and the lambda receives the element second in C# but element first in Motoi:

```scheme
;; C# .Aggregate(seed, f) -> Motoi fold. Element arrives first in Motoi.
(fold (lambda (elem acc) (+ acc elem)) 0 (list 1 2 3 4 5))
```

---

## More region-one strays: the Elixir and functional-language family

Functional languages beyond Haskell bring their own vocabulary. Elixir, for instance, carries an `Enum` module with verbs like `Enum.map`, `Enum.filter`, `Enum.reduce`, `Enum.chunk_every`, and `Enum.flat_map`. The `|>` pipe operator threads data through a chain. None of those are Motoi verbs by those names — `Enum.map` is region one, language Elixir — but the operations are all present:

```scheme
;; Elixir Enum.map / Enum.filter -> plain map / filter. Same ideas, my spelling.
(map (lambda (x) (+ x 10))
     (filter (lambda (x) (> x 2)) (list 1 2 3 4 5)))
```

Elixir's `Enum.chunk_every(xs, n)` splits a list into chunks of size n. Motoi has no single verb for this, but the construction is not long — a named let accumulates the chunks:

```scheme
;; Elixir Enum.chunk_every(xs, 3) -> hand-built chunker in named-let.
(let chunk ((xs (list 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8)) (n 3) (acc '()))
  (cond ((null? xs) (reverse acc))
        ((< (length xs) n)
         (reverse (cons xs acc)))
        (else (chunk (drop xs n) n (cons (take xs n) acc)))))
```

Elixir's `Enum.frequencies` tallies a list into a map of element to count — the exact pattern that Motoi's hash-table update idiom handles:

```scheme
;; Elixir Enum.frequencies -> hash-table-update!/default counter. Mine.
(define freq-table (hash-table))
(for-each (lambda (w)
            (hash-table-update!/default freq-table w (lambda (n) (+ n 1)) 0))
          (list \"a\" \"b\" \"a\" \"c\" \"b\" \"a\"))
(list (hash-table-ref/default freq-table \"a\" 0)
      (hash-table-ref/default freq-table \"b\" 0))
```

The rule to carry out of this chapter: the edge of your language is knowledge, not embarrassment. When a verb is not yours, say so plainly, name *why* it is not yours — other dialect, excluded standard, or pure invention — and then, in the chapters ahead, learn to find the verb that is. What Motoi *actually is*, in positive terms — the full inventory of what lives inside the edge — is the whole of the next chapter.")
